-*- coding:utf-8 -*-

alfe-load-form-argument-not-evaluated — the bootstrap's (load …) interception passes the argument unevaluated
=============================================================================================================

Reported from: SCHMS+ CAD test harness (sncf-reseau/src/schms,
tests/test-cad.lsp) run via `alfe --bricscad --mode batch` on macOS,
BricsCAD V26.

First observed on alfe 1.6.0; **re-confirmed reproducing on alfe 1.6.14
/ clautolisp 1.6.17** (BricsCAD V26, macOS), which is what the "Root
cause" section below describes.

The literal-argument case was re-checked at the same time and WORKS:

    (load "/abs/path/outils-test-cad.lsp")   => LITERAL-OK
    (vl-catch-all-apply 'load (list p))      => APPLY=OK
    (load p)                                 => bad argument type <P>

That matters for the triage note at the end of this issue: the failure
described by cad-vl-filename-directory-and-load — an in-file `(load
<literal absolute path>)` — does NOT reproduce here, so if it was real
it is a separate problem, and if it was a misdiagnosis of this bug then
that issue can simply be closed.

Summary
-------

Inside a file loaded by `-l`, and in any `-x` request form, a top-level
`(load X)` where X is anything other than a STRING LITERAL fails: the
bootstrap hands `X` to its own loader **unevaluated**.

    (load "/abs/path/file.lsp")                        => works
    (setq p "/abs/path/file.lsp") (load p)             => FAILS
    (load (strcat repo "/tests/runner.lsp"))           => FAILS
    (vl-catch-all-apply 'load (list p))                => works

The error names the source expression, not its value, which makes it
read like a type error in the caller:

    bad argument type <P> ; expected <STRING> at [findfile]
    bad argument type <(STRCAT ZZ:REPO \"/tests/test-runner-commun.lsp\")>
      ; expected <STRING> at [findfile]

Minimal reproduction
--------------------

    $ cat > /tmp/mini.lsp <<'EOF'
    (setq zz:dir  (vl-filename-directory "/tmp/a/b/file.lsp"))
    (setq zz:repo (vl-filename-directory zz:dir))
    (princ (strcat "\nDIR=[" zz:dir "] REPO=[" zz:repo "]"))
    (load (strcat zz:repo "/some-existing-file.lsp"))
    (princ "\nLOADED-OK")
    EOF
    $ alfe -norc --bricscad --mode batch -l /tmp/mini.lsp

DIR/REPO print correctly (so the path computation is fine) and the
`(load …)` on the next line fails. Replacing it with a string literal,
or with `(vl-catch-all-apply 'load (list <same expression>))`, works.

Root cause
----------

`autolisp-front-end/source/runtime/autolisp-bootstrap.lsp`:

  - `autolisp-source-load-run-body-impl` (line ~461) evaluates every
    top-level form of a `-l` file through `autolisp-eval-request-form`,
    not through plain `eval`.

  - `autolisp-eval-request-form` (line ~703) dispatches on
    `autolisp-load-form-p`, which is a purely syntactic test — "car of
    the form is the symbol LOAD":

        (defun autolisp-load-form-p (form)
          (and (listp form) form
               (= (type (car form)) 'SYM)
               (= (strcase (vl-symbol-name (car form))) "LOAD")))

  - `autolisp-eval-load-form` (line ~688) then passes `(cadr form)`
    straight to `autolisp-source-load` / `autolisp-internal-protocol-load-p`:

        ((= (length form) 2)
         (autolisp-source-load (cadr form)))

    `(cadr form)` is the *unevaluated* argument. For `(load p)` that is
    the symbol `P`; for `(load (strcat a b))` it is the list
    `(STRCAT A B)`. Both then fail inside `findfile`, which is where the
    reported error comes from.

The same dispatch is duplicated in the emitted bootstrap text in
`autolisp-front-end/source/file-protocol.lisp` (~line 1187).

Expected
--------

`load` is an ordinary AutoLISP function; its argument is evaluated on
every engine (clautolisp, BricsCAD, AutoCAD). The interception exists to
route source loads through alfe's own loader, which is fine — but it
must evaluate the argument first:

    ((= (length form) 2)
     (autolisp-source-load (eval (cadr form))))

and likewise for the 3-argument (onfailure) branch and for the
`autolisp-internal-protocol-load-p` guards, which currently test the
unevaluated argument against a `*protocol-request-*.lsp` wildcard and so
never match a computed path.

Care is needed for the onfailure argument: `(load path onfailure)` must
evaluate `path` but the failure value is a datum, matching the AutoLISP
contract.

Why it matters
--------------

Computing a path and loading it is the normal way an AutoLISP harness or
application bootstraps itself — there is no `*load-truename*`, so a file
derives its siblings from a variable. SCHMS+ does it in production too
(`schms_active_objet` does `(load (com_way (schms_nomfichier_script …)))`,
src-vlx/objet_workflow.lsp:16); that one happens to survive because it
sits inside a `defun` body rather than at top level, so it is not routed
through the request path. The divergence is therefore silent and
position-dependent: the same expression works or fails depending on
whether it is nested in a function.

Workaround in use on the SCHMS+ side (tests/test-cad.lsp,
tests/test-unitaire.lsp):

    (vl-catch-all-apply (function load) (list (strcat *tu:repo-dir* "/…")))

Related
-------

`cad-vl-filename-directory-and-load.issue` reached the same symptom from
the other end ("in-file native (load <abs-path>) fails under BricsCAD's
load shadow in some contexts") and asked to be retitled/triaged; its
`vl-filename-directory` angle can be dropped and it can be closed in
favour of this issue, which pins the mechanism. Note that the case it
describes — an in-file `(load <literal absolute path>)` — should NOT be
affected by the bug described here; if it genuinely failed, there is a
second, distinct problem still to explain.

Suggested test
--------------

A conformance case asserting that, for each backend, a `-l` file
containing

    (setq p "<abs path>")
    (load p)

loads the target, and that `(load (strcat …))` does too.
